.he "disk2 (4)                   10/15/26                    disk2 (4)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
disk2
- a hard disk on a CompuPro DISK 2 controller
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
The
.bd
disk2
device reads and writes a hard disk attached to a CompuPro DISK 2
controller.  The board drives Shugart-compatible disks, and the manual
names the families it was sold for: the Memorex 100 series, the
Fujitsu 2300 series - an eight inch drive, 11.7 and 23.4 megabytes
unformatted - and the Shugart SA4000 series.  The SA4000 is the odd
one.  It is compatible in the sense that matters here, stepping its
head and moving its data the way the eight inch drives do, but it is a
fourteen inch drive, and two read/write heads read each of its disk
surfaces rather than one, so a four head drive has two surfaces and an
eight head drive four, each head reaching all 202 cylinders.  Its
track also holds more sectors than an eight inch drive's, 18000 bytes
of them.  Nothing in this driver has to know any of that: the sector
count is the label's, and the one difference the manual speaks of is a
jumper that sets the write clock's phase.  Disks of this family are
also what the HDCA drives.  Four hang off a controller, and the bit
rate is in the order of 5 MHz, the drive being what decides it.  It is
the simple one of the two CompuPro hard disk controllers.  A finite
state machine on the board searches for a sector's header and moves
its data, but the seek is the host's and not the board's - the head is
stepped a track at a time - and every transfer's address comes from a
second board, the SELECTOR CHANNEL, which the driver programs just
before each command.

Two ports are the board's own.  0xc8 is the control register written
and the status register read.  0xc9 is a data register written and one
step pulse read: a read of the data port is not data, it moves the
head, in the direction the control register's direction bit names.
Which of four data registers 0xc9 writes to is the control register's
OP1 and OP0 bits - the drive and head selection, the cylinder, the
head, and the sector to search for - and a command is that register
with the RUN bit set.  The SELECTOR CHANNEL answers at 0xe0: four
writes give the 24 bit physical address and the mode of the transfer
to come, and a read releases it.

The device number packs two things.  The major number is 7, and the
low two bits of the minor number name the drive.

.in +5
.nf
7/0  d2a        7/2  d2c
7/1  d2b        7/3  d2d
.in -5
.fi

Those four appear again 64 higher, as their whole-disk slices: 7/64
is d2ac, the drive at cylinder 0 and unrotated, so that its block 0 is
physical block 0 and reading it reads the disk's label.

The geometry is not part of the name.  It comes off the disk, from the
label
.bd
mkfs
writes into the second half of the boot sector: a magic number, the
number of cylinders, heads and sectors, the rotation below, and the
size of the file system made on it.  The driver reads that label when
the device is opened, so there is no table of drive sizes to disagree
with it, and a disk carrying no label cannot be opened at all.

A block number is a file system block, and the driver turns it into a
cylinder, a head and a sector.  Block 0 is not at cylinder 0: the
volume is rolled, so that the superblock and the inode list sit in the
middle of the platter rather than at its edge.  How far is the label's
to say, which is the same roll the HDDMA derives from a drive's size.

The seek is the driver's work, and it does not spin to wait for it.
The board has no seek command and no timer for one: the host sends the
step pulses, reading 0xc9 for each, and a drive that takes pulses
faster than it steps - the manual calls it fast seek - accumulates
them and moves when it is ready.  When the last pulse is out the
driver issues the board's NULL command, which does nothing to the disk
at all.  The manual provides it so that a command can be left waiting
for the drive's SEEK COMPLETE, which is what says the head has
arrived.  A seek is therefore two commands, and the time the arm takes
to settle is time the machine spends on something else.

The board's interrupt is the manual's "done" flag.  A command that
finishes pulls the vectored interrupt line its J7..J14 jumper selects,
and the line stays down until the host writes the control register,
which is what makes the status register transparent to read again.  So
every command the driver issues is also the acknowledgement of the one
before it, and there is no separate end-of-interrupt to give the card.
The driver names no line: the kernel finds which one the card is
jumpered to at boot.  A request is issued and left, and the status
check, the retry and the next buffer all run in the interrupt handler.

A read or a write that comes back with ATTENTION, TIME OUT, CRC or
OVER RUN set in the status has failed, and so has a write that the
drive reports as a fault.  The driver re-issues it, up to ten times,
and halfway through the ten it steps the head all the way out to track
0 first, since a head that has lost its place is the usual reason a
command fails.  A board that has not answered for a second is a
request failed with ENXIO rather than a machine that waits for ever,
and the head's position is a guess from then on, so the next request
homes the drive before it does anything else.

The driver offers no raw command interface, so a disk on this
controller is not formatted through Micronix.  A drive must be
formatted elsewhere first, and then given a Micronix label by
.bd
mkfs
, which is what makes it a volume this system can mount.
.sp 2
.in -5
